1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,263
That's the second of the four
questions that open Chapter 14A.

2
00:00:08,303 --> 00:00:13,354
Look at what you're given. Not a commit,
one of the saved snapshots of the project.

3
00:00:13,394 --> 00:00:17,954
Not a file name you can be sure of,
because the file may have been renamed.

4
00:00:17,994 --> 00:00:22,922
Not a line number, because the line is
gone. You have a string that used to be in

5
00:00:22,962 --> 00:00:24,701
the code and isn't there now.

6
00:00:25,041 --> 00:00:29,758
You can't open what is missing. You can't
blame it, which means asking who last

7
00:00:29,798 --> 00:00:34,515
changed each line. And you can't grep for
it in the working tree, the files you

8
00:00:34,555 --> 00:00:39,393
edit. The history still has it. This video
gives you four ways to ask the history

9
00:00:39,433 --> 00:00:44,151
about content: follow a file through its
renames, search for the commit where a

10
00:00:44,191 --> 00:00:49,029
string appeared or vanished, trace a
function line by line, and bring back a file

11
00:00:49,069 --> 00:00:52,160
that was deleted. Keep that
vanished string in mind.

12
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:53,003
It comes back.

13
00:00:54,843 --> 00:00:58,387
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

14
00:00:58,427 --> 00:01:02,971
In the previous two videos your queries
were about commits as members of sets.

15
00:01:03,011 --> 00:01:06,026
Today the query is about
content inside the commits.

16
00:01:06,066 --> 00:01:10,844
All four tools are still git log. They're
stage-one and stage-two options from the

17
00:01:10,884 --> 00:01:15,603
model you already have: stage one decides
which commits are walked, and stage two

18
00:01:15,643 --> 00:01:17,014
which of them are shown.

19
00:01:17,354 --> 00:01:23,743
Hold on to the fact that carries the
chapter. History stores snapshots and parent

20
00:01:23,783 --> 00:01:30,013
links, nothing else. Git records no renames
and no "this line came from there".

21
00:01:30,053 --> 00:01:36,601
Every answer you get today is computed by
comparing snapshots, with heuristics that

22
00:01:36,641 --> 00:01:43,109
have thresholds and blind spots. For each
tool, I'll tell you what it computes, so

23
00:01:43,149 --> 00:01:48,427
that you know when its answer is a
fact and when it's an inference.

24
00:01:48,767 --> 00:01:53,625
The repository is scorekit again.
One detail matters today: the file

25
00:01:53,665 --> 00:01:59,603
scorekit/metrics.py began life as scorer.py
at the top level and was renamed twice.

26
00:02:01,443 --> 00:02:05,744
follow one file across a rename
and state the limits of --follow;

27
00:02:06,084 --> 00:02:12,363
choose between -S and -G, and construct
a commit that only one of them reports;

28
00:02:12,703 --> 00:02:17,271
trace the history of a function
or a line range with git log -L;

29
00:02:17,611 --> 00:02:21,587
find the commit that deleted a file
and restore the file from its parent;

30
00:02:21,927 --> 00:02:24,830
find a file that exists
only on another branch.

31
00:02:26,670 --> 00:02:30,669
Following a file. git log --
<path> matches a path, not a file.

32
00:02:30,709 --> 00:02:35,798
When the file had another name, the history
stops at the commit that gave it the

33
00:02:35,838 --> 00:02:36,632
current name.

34
00:02:36,972 --> 00:02:41,763
--follow runs rename detection at each
step and continues under the old name.

35
00:02:42,103 --> 00:02:46,144
Its limits are all consequences
of how rename detection works.

36
00:02:46,184 --> 00:02:51,279
First, one path only: the manual says
--follow "works only for a single file".

37
00:02:51,319 --> 00:02:56,150
Second, the heuristic can miss: a rename
combined with a rewrite below the

38
00:02:56,190 --> 00:03:01,285
similarity threshold ends the trail.
Third, it's not git log with full history

39
00:03:01,325 --> 00:03:01,943
semantics.

40
00:03:02,283 --> 00:03:07,345
The manual says of the log.follow setting
that it "does not work well on non-linear

41
00:03:07,385 --> 00:03:12,508
history". The release notes of Git 2.56
record an improvement for histories in which

42
00:03:12,548 --> 00:03:16,688
the tracked path is renamed differently
in several lines of history.

43
00:03:16,728 --> 00:03:21,790
That version was not run here. If you know
the old names, plain path limiting takes

44
00:03:21,830 --> 00:03:24,064
several paths and needs no heuristic.

45
00:03:24,404 --> 00:03:29,473
The pickaxe. In one sentence: -S with a
string finds commits that changed how many

46
00:03:29,513 --> 00:03:34,644
times that string occurs in a file, and -G
with a regular expression, a pattern for

47
00:03:34,684 --> 00:03:39,752
matching text, finds commits whose patch
has an added or removed line matching the

48
00:03:39,792 --> 00:03:43,428
pattern. The patch is the
commit's diff against its parent.

49
00:03:43,768 --> 00:03:48,905
Precisely, both are stage-two filters.
The documentation says that -S "detects

50
00:03:48,945 --> 00:03:54,347
filepairs whose preimage and postimage have
different number of occurrences of the

51
00:03:54,387 --> 00:03:59,259
specified block of text. By definition,
it will not detect in-file moves."

52
00:03:59,299 --> 00:04:04,568
And -G "detects filepairs whose textual
diff has an added or a deleted line that

53
00:04:04,608 --> 00:04:10,143
matches the given regular expression." The
argument of -S is a literal string unless

54
00:04:10,183 --> 00:04:15,187
you give --pickaxe-regex. The argument
of -G is always a regular expression.

55
00:04:15,527 --> 00:04:21,046
When to use which: -S for "when was
this introduced, when was it removed".

56
00:04:21,086 --> 00:04:25,177
-G for "which commits touched
lines that mention this".

57
00:04:25,217 --> 00:04:29,834
-S is cheap. -G costs more,
because every candidate is diffed.

58
00:04:30,174 --> 00:04:35,245
Line history. git log -L follows a range
of lines backwards and prints, for each

59
00:04:35,285 --> 00:04:39,971
commit that touched those lines, the
part of its patch that concerns them.

60
00:04:40,011 --> 00:04:45,145
The range is start, end, colon, file. Or
colon, function name, colon, file, for a

61
00:04:45,185 --> 00:04:46,104
whole function.

62
00:04:46,444 --> 00:04:51,210
How the range is found matters. A number
is a line number in the starting revision,

63
00:04:51,250 --> 00:04:55,670
HEAD, your current commit, unless you
name another, not in your working tree.

64
00:04:55,710 --> 00:05:00,071
The function name is a regular expression
matched against the lines that Git

65
00:05:00,111 --> 00:05:04,820
considers function headers, the same lines
it prints after the @@ in hunk headers.

66
00:05:05,160 --> 00:05:09,617
A hunk is one block of changed lines in
a diff. The range runs to the next such

67
00:05:09,657 --> 00:05:14,113
line. For languages where the default
guess is poor, a diff attribute selects a

68
00:05:14,153 --> 00:05:18,382
language-specific pattern. You meet
that attribute in the attributes video.

69
00:05:18,722 --> 00:05:23,748
A deleted file. A file that isn't in the
working tree can't be opened, blamed or

70
00:05:23,788 --> 00:05:28,180
grepped. Its history is still there.
You find the deleting commit with

71
00:05:28,220 --> 00:05:33,055
--diff-filter=D. On screen, today's
deleting commit, d9d075d, and its parent.

72
00:05:33,095 --> 00:05:38,248
The content lives in the parent of the
deleting commit, not in the deleting commit

73
00:05:38,288 --> 00:05:38,691
itself.

74
00:05:40,531 --> 00:05:45,391
Now a picture to keep. The textbook gives
the pickaxe an analogy from a warehouse.

75
00:05:45,731 --> 00:05:50,798
-S is an inventory count. It compares
the number of items before and after and

76
00:05:50,838 --> 00:05:54,202
reports a difference. -G is
a goods-movement ledger.

77
00:05:54,242 --> 00:05:59,702
It reports every delivery and every removal,
including the day one crate left and an

78
00:05:59,742 --> 00:06:01,273
identical crate arrived.

79
00:06:01,613 --> 00:06:07,641
Try it now, on paper. Thirty seconds: the
pass mark changes from 0.7 to 0.75, and

80
00:06:07,681 --> 00:06:13,859
the name PASS_MARK stays. Write down what
the count sees, and what the ledger sees.

81
00:06:13,899 --> 00:06:15,432
Then say your answer.

82
00:06:20,272 --> 00:06:25,611
Here's the answer. The name PASS_MARK is
on the shelf once before and once after.

83
00:06:25,651 --> 00:06:27,802
The inventory count sees nothing.

84
00:06:28,142 --> 00:06:30,605
The ledger sees a line
removed and a line added.

85
00:06:30,945 --> 00:06:35,619
The analogy breaks at files. Both
options look at one file at a time.

86
00:06:35,659 --> 00:06:41,290
A string that moves from one file to another
is reported by -S as well, because one

87
00:06:41,330 --> 00:06:44,501
file lost an occurrence
and another gained one.

88
00:06:46,841 --> 00:06:51,065
Before the demonstration, decide: your
question is "who removed the call".

89
00:06:51,105 --> 00:06:52,045
Count, or ledger?

90
00:06:54,385 --> 00:06:59,575
Count. A removal changes the number of
occurrences, so -S is the right tool.

91
00:06:59,615 --> 00:07:01,708
You'll use it in a few minutes.

92
00:07:03,548 --> 00:07:09,198
Row one: the call is added. Both report it.
Row two: the line is edited and the call

93
00:07:09,238 --> 00:07:12,789
stays. The count is unchanged,
so only -G reports it.

94
00:07:12,829 --> 00:07:17,057
Row three is the one almost
everyone gets wrong the first time.

95
00:07:17,097 --> 00:07:21,121
The total across the repository
is one before and one after.

96
00:07:21,161 --> 00:07:26,812
But the count is per file: metrics.py goes
from one to zero and text.py from zero to

97
00:07:26,852 --> 00:07:31,418
one. Both options report it. Row
four: the call is removed for good.

98
00:07:41,458 --> 00:07:46,661
Six commits, and the oldest is "Rename
the scorer module to metrics".

99
00:07:46,701 --> 00:07:50,991
The history of the path ends
where the path got its name.

100
00:07:51,031 --> 00:07:56,081
Predict: how many more commits will
--follow show? Say it out loud.

101
00:07:56,121 --> 00:07:56,841
I'll wait.

102
00:08:01,681 --> 00:08:06,672
Three more: the move into the package, and
the two commits from the time the file

103
00:08:06,712 --> 00:08:11,827
was scorer.py. Now ask for the chain of
names. --diff-filter=AR keeps additions and

104
00:08:11,867 --> 00:08:12,323
renames.

105
00:08:12,663 --> 00:08:19,148
Read it from the bottom: added as scorer.py
in b250238, moved to scorekit/scorer.py

106
00:08:19,188 --> 00:08:23,392
in d38a5aa, renamed to
scorekit/metrics.py in 18bb23e.

107
00:08:23,432 --> 00:08:28,737
The R100 is a similarity score of
100 percent, computed, not stored.

108
00:08:29,077 --> 00:08:30,342
Now the first limit.

109
00:08:30,682 --> 00:08:36,147
Exactly one pathspec, one path pattern. And
the alternative that needs no heuristic,

110
00:08:36,187 --> 00:08:37,720
when you know the names:

111
00:08:38,060 --> 00:08:39,275
The same nine commits.

112
00:08:43,615 --> 00:08:50,038
Three against five. -G reports two commits
that -S doesn't, and the first of them,

113
00:08:50,078 --> 00:08:55,948
389337a, is the one a person asking
"who changed the pass mark" wants most.

114
00:08:55,988 --> 00:08:57,840
Here's why -S missed it.

115
00:08:58,180 --> 00:09:04,204
The value changed and the name stayed. Point
at the counts from git grep -c: one in

116
00:09:04,244 --> 00:09:08,076
config.py before, one after.
For -S nothing happened.

117
00:09:08,116 --> 00:09:12,167
The line was removed and added:
for -G it matches twice.

118
00:09:12,207 --> 00:09:18,085
The second extra commit, 9c8df98, is the
formatter, which re-indented a line that

119
00:09:18,125 --> 00:09:21,518
mentions PASS_MARK. That's
the noise -G brings.

120
00:09:21,858 --> 00:09:26,514
Now the regression of this chapter, the
change that broke something that worked.

121
00:09:26,554 --> 00:09:31,327
It's a removal, so -S is the right tool.
The call .lower() no longer exists in the

122
00:09:31,367 --> 00:09:31,797
sources.

123
00:09:32,137 --> 00:09:37,005
This is the diagram, as output. Three
commits changed the count: the one that

124
00:09:37,045 --> 00:09:42,232
introduced the call, the one that moved
the function to another file, and d926d3c,

125
00:09:42,272 --> 00:09:47,523
where it disappeared for good. -G adds a
fourth, 8e2cac1, which edited the line and

126
00:09:47,563 --> 00:09:51,539
kept the call. Add -p to read the
evidence in the same command.

127
00:09:51,879 --> 00:09:56,240
"Speed up normalize with a precompiled
pattern", by Ravi Menon on the tenth.

128
00:09:56,280 --> 00:10:01,045
A string vanished, and now you have its
commit. The patch shows only the files that

129
00:10:01,085 --> 00:10:05,909
matched, and --pickaxe-all shows the whole
commit. Don't conclude anything about the

130
00:10:05,949 --> 00:10:10,541
person yet. You have a commit to read,
and the method for reading it is the next

131
00:10:10,581 --> 00:10:10,888
video.

132
00:10:11,228 --> 00:10:15,031
One more property: the pickaxe searches
only the commits of stage one.

133
00:10:15,371 --> 00:10:21,335
Nothing from HEAD, and two commits with
--all. Use --all when the code may live on

134
00:10:21,375 --> 00:10:27,192
another branch, and --reflog when it may
have been rebased away, replaced by new

135
00:10:27,232 --> 00:10:27,704
copies.

136
00:10:28,044 --> 00:10:33,518
-s suppresses the patches and leaves the
list. Nine commits touched the file.

137
00:10:33,558 --> 00:10:39,247
Four touched this function. The oldest,
bb5eb57, predates both renames, which -L

138
00:10:39,287 --> 00:10:40,679
followed on its own.

139
00:10:41,019 --> 00:10:42,744
One line works the same way.

140
00:10:43,084 --> 00:10:48,151
Line 2 of config.py: the pass mark, with
the hunk of each commit that changed it.

141
00:10:48,191 --> 00:10:53,195
And the function at the centre of the
regression, limited to its two most recent

142
00:10:53,235 --> 00:10:53,699
changes.

143
00:10:57,039 --> 00:11:01,958
Read from the bottom. Ravi's commit
replaced the line that lowercased the text.

144
00:11:01,998 --> 00:11:07,231
The formatter then changed quotes and
indentation. -L shows deleted lines, which git

145
00:11:07,271 --> 00:11:10,997
blame can't. The errors are
explicit, and worth seeing once.

146
00:11:11,337 --> 00:11:16,493
A range beyond the end of the file, a
function name that matches no header, and -L

147
00:11:16,533 --> 00:11:19,407
combined with a pathspec,
which isn't allowed.

148
00:11:19,747 --> 00:11:25,894
One deletion: scorekit/bleu.py, in d9d075d.
If you remember only part of the name,

149
00:11:25,934 --> 00:11:28,233
use a pathspec with a wildcard.

150
00:11:28,574 --> 00:11:33,713
The first command shows the history under
the last name, with the deletion on top.

151
00:11:33,753 --> 00:11:38,955
The second also reaches d369eac, the commit
that created the file as bleu.py at the

152
00:11:38,995 --> 00:11:44,008
top level. The two dashes are required:
without them Git must decide whether the

153
00:11:44,048 --> 00:11:49,251
word is a revision or a path, and for a
file that no longer exists it gives up with

154
00:11:49,291 --> 00:11:52,472
"ambiguous argument". The
deleting commit says why.

155
00:11:52,812 --> 00:11:57,701
A reason in the message: nothing imports
it, and ROUGE-L covers the use case.

156
00:11:57,741 --> 00:12:01,223
Now predict: does this print
the file? Say it out loud.

157
00:12:06,063 --> 00:12:11,210
No. The path doesn't exist in the deleting
commit. That's what deleting means.

158
00:12:11,250 --> 00:12:13,138
The content is in its parent.

159
00:12:13,478 --> 00:12:17,968
This is the first state-changing command of
the video, and it carries the label that

160
00:12:18,008 --> 00:12:21,474
Chapter 11 gives it. Answer the
five questions before running it.

161
00:12:21,514 --> 00:12:24,602
What it changes: it writes the
file into the working tree.

162
00:12:24,642 --> 00:12:29,132
What it can destroy: it overwrites an existing
file of that name without asking, and

163
00:12:29,172 --> 00:12:32,152
uncommitted content in the
working tree is in no object.

164
00:12:32,492 --> 00:12:37,648
How to preview: git show <commit>~1:<path>
prints the content, and git status tells

165
00:12:37,688 --> 00:12:42,781
you whether a file of that name exists.
How to recover: an overwritten uncommitted

166
00:12:42,821 --> 00:12:47,914
file can't be recovered from Git. When it's
appropriate: when no such file exists,

167
00:12:47,954 --> 00:12:51,481
or when you have decided to
replace it. Here none exists.

168
00:12:51,821 --> 00:12:56,459
The file comes back untracked: it's in the
working tree, and Git doesn't track it

169
00:12:56,499 --> 00:13:01,079
yet. Git hasn't undone the deletion. You
have a copy of old content to add or to

170
00:13:01,119 --> 00:13:01,541
discard.

171
00:13:01,881 --> 00:13:05,602
A file can also be "missing" because
it was never on this branch.

172
00:13:05,942 --> 00:13:11,416
--all -- <path> searches the history of
every ref, every branch and tag, for the

173
00:13:11,456 --> 00:13:16,791
path. git branch --contains, with -a to
include remote-tracking branches, your

174
00:13:16,831 --> 00:13:22,098
records of branches in other repositories,
then says where that commit lives:

175
00:13:22,138 --> 00:13:22,925
feat/report.

176
00:13:24,765 --> 00:13:26,413
Five mistakes to watch for.

177
00:13:26,753 --> 00:13:32,238
"The file has only six commits of history."
Root cause: git log -- <path> matches a

178
00:13:32,278 --> 00:13:37,562
path, and the file had other names before;
without --follow or the old names the

179
00:13:37,602 --> 00:13:39,426
history stops at the rename.

180
00:13:39,766 --> 00:13:45,422
Using -S to find who changed a value. Root
cause: the name occurs once before and

181
00:13:45,462 --> 00:13:51,048
once after, so the occurrence count did
not change; -G matches the changed line.

182
00:13:51,388 --> 00:13:55,076
Searching for a whole statement
copied from an old file.

183
00:13:55,116 --> 00:14:00,602
Root cause: the string stops matching as
soon as anyone edits its tail, and -S then

184
00:14:00,642 --> 00:14:06,060
reports that edit as the removal. Search
for the shortest string that is specific.

185
00:14:06,400 --> 00:14:11,067
git show <deleting-commit>:<path> fails.
Root cause: the file is absent from the

186
00:14:11,107 --> 00:14:15,127
snapshot of the commit that deleted
it; the content is in the parent.

187
00:14:15,467 --> 00:14:20,500
Giving -L a line number from the editor.
Root cause: the number is a line in the

188
00:14:20,540 --> 00:14:25,636
starting revision, HEAD by default, not
in a working tree with uncommitted edits.

189
00:14:27,476 --> 00:14:32,018
Now, out of the lab. An LLM application
team has an alert that counts a warning

190
00:14:32,058 --> 00:14:36,717
string in the service logs. The alert has
been silent for a week, and the on-call

191
00:14:36,757 --> 00:14:41,183
engineer wants to know whether the
condition stopped occurring or the warning

192
00:14:41,223 --> 00:14:42,459
stopped being printed.

193
00:14:42,799 --> 00:14:46,926
She searches the history for the warning
text with -S, across all branches, with a

194
00:14:46,966 --> 00:14:51,093
short specific fragment and not the whole
message. The newest commit in the result

195
00:14:51,133 --> 00:14:55,311
is the one where the count dropped to zero.
She reads its patch in the same command

196
00:14:55,351 --> 00:14:59,224
with -p, and the message explains that
warnings were consolidated under a new

197
00:14:59,264 --> 00:14:59,630
wording.

198
00:14:59,970 --> 00:15:03,073
The alert was matching a
string that no longer exists.

199
00:15:03,113 --> 00:15:07,088
The fix is in the alert rule, and
the incident note cites the commit.

200
00:15:07,128 --> 00:15:11,860
It took one query, because the question
was a transition and she asked a tool that

201
00:15:11,900 --> 00:15:15,352
searches transitions. That's
the opening question, answered.

202
00:15:17,192 --> 00:15:25,248
Your turn. Do Lab 11.2, "Find when a string
was removed, with -S and then with -G",

203
00:15:25,288 --> 00:15:29,053
in lab-manual/m11-history-forensics.md.

204
00:15:29,393 --> 00:15:34,067
Before each of the two searches, predict
how many commits it will report and which

205
00:15:34,107 --> 00:15:38,782
commit will be the newest. The lab's failure
scenario has you search with a string

206
00:15:38,822 --> 00:15:43,439
that is too long. Predict what -S will
report as "the removal" before you run it.

207
00:15:43,779 --> 00:15:50,882
The challenge is Lab 11.7, "Line history
with git log -L", in the same file.

208
00:15:52,722 --> 00:15:53,999
Pause and answer out loud.

209
00:15:58,839 --> 00:16:03,210
A strong answer defines each option by what
it computes, a change in the number of

210
00:16:03,250 --> 00:16:07,729
occurrences per file against a match on
added or removed lines, and doesn't describe

211
00:16:07,769 --> 00:16:12,032
them as "a string search and a regex
search". It gives a concrete commit for the

212
00:16:12,072 --> 00:16:14,775
gap, and can say why the
count didn't change in it.

213
00:16:15,115 --> 00:16:18,772
It names a realistic source of noise
and what it costs the investigator.

214
00:16:18,812 --> 00:16:22,930
It adds the scope rule, that both search
only the walked commits, and it finishes

215
00:16:22,970 --> 00:16:26,935
with the choice: which of the two fits
"introduced or removed", and which fits

216
00:16:26,975 --> 00:16:27,448
"touched".

217
00:16:29,288 --> 00:16:32,215
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

218
00:16:32,555 --> 00:16:38,242
git log -- <path> follows a path; --follow
follows one file through renames by a

219
00:16:38,282 --> 00:16:39,960
heuristic that can miss.

220
00:16:40,300 --> 00:16:46,365
-S reports a change in the number of
occurrences in a file; -G reports any added or

221
00:16:46,405 --> 00:16:48,278
removed line that matches.

222
00:16:48,618 --> 00:16:53,807
git log -L gives the history of a function
or a line range, including lines that

223
00:16:53,847 --> 00:16:54,656
were deleted.

224
00:16:54,996 --> 00:17:00,440
A deleted file is found with --diff-filter=D
and its content is in the parent of the

225
00:17:00,480 --> 00:17:01,484
deleting commit.

226
00:17:01,824 --> 00:17:08,609
A search from HEAD does not see other
branches; --all and --reflog widen the walk.

